deep.reconstruction:
Context (pp. 16 and 18). Grothendieck has enlarged the Teichmüller–Poincaré groupoid of a curve to a 'Teichmüller–Galois' category, which is no longer a groupoid. Its morphisms are, essentially, finite étale coverings between open curves (equivalently, isotopy classes of finite coverings of punctured compact surfaces), and they link curves of different combinatorial types (g,ν). This page names a type of morphism he has not yet admitted: open inclusions (23) X = Y \ T ↪ Y, where T is a finite set of points. Read in the direction of Y, this is 'filling holes' (bouchage des trous); in moduli terms it corresponds to forgetting or erasing marked points, M_{g,ν+|T|} → M_{g,ν}.

If both finite étale maps and open immersions are admitted, the category must be closed under composition. Composites can come in either order: an étale cover of an open subset Y' ⊂ Y followed by Y' ↪ Y, or an open immersion followed by a cover. So the general morphism X → Y is a map of open curves that fits into a commutative square (24):
- X ↪ X̂ over Y ↪ Ŷ, where X̂ and Ŷ are the smooth compactifications;
- X̂ → Ŷ is finite and possibly ramified;
- X → Y is itself étale (the reading is uncertain), but no longer finite.

Put S = X̂ \ X and T = Ŷ \ Y (the manuscript slips and writes Ŷ − T). Since X maps into Y, (25) S ⊃ X̂|T, the inverse image of T. The inclusion can be strict. The puncture set S consists of:
- S_0 = X̂|T, the points over T;
- the ramification points of X̂ → Ŷ lying over points of Y. These must be removed if X → Y is to be étale;
- arbitrary further unramified points of S not in X̂|T.

The last line makes exactly this point: one must adjoin not only the ramification points and the points of X̂|T, but also points of S not in X̂|T.

On p. 18 he defines isotopy for such morphisms through Aut(X̂,S) × Aut(Ŷ,T). He then observes that an isotopy class of such morphisms no longer has a canonical factorization as (hole-filling inclusion) followed by (finite cover branched only over distinguished points). He concludes that he no longer sees how to interpret his 'belles classes', or their profinite completions, in terms of abstract topological groups over ℂ.

The problem served is the 'programme à l'horizon': an algebraic or combinatorial description of the Galois–Teichmüller category with Gal(Q̄/Q) acting on it, now extended to include point-erasing maps.
